{
  "dom_id": "step-q65276",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "What is the minimum value of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-2?$",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, shift it by $2$ units **down.**\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254493/assets/d14843fdb32ab94224243fe2ff26c281195faa57e1e025d6157d301971044732.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the minimum value is $y=-3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe know that $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ has a minimum value of $-1.$ \n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubtracting $2$ from both sides of the above inequality gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1-2 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2 \\\\ -3 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2$ is $-3.$",
